    "K_t": "Evidence:\n[1] The new criticism held that a work should be treated as though it were contemporary and anonymous whether it was a text by Tacitus or Hemingway.\n[2] Also very influential were the critical essays of T. S. Eliot, such as \"Tradition and the Individual Talent\" and \"Hamlet and His Problems\", in which Eliot developed his notion of the \"objective correlative\".\n[3] Hamlet and His Problems is an essay written by T.S. Eliot in 1919 that offers a critical reading of \"Hamlet\".\n[4] The essay is also an example of Eliot's use of what became known as new criticism."
